Draw menus and dialogs at the radius the design uses.

The app menu, its submenus, its touch variant and every dialog were at 12px.
The design draws a card at 16 to 18, and now so does everything else in the
browser: aerium-settings-style.patch gives the settings cards an 18px radius and
dialogs 16, and the new tab page's own panel is 16. A 12px native menu opening
over a 16px WebUI dialog reads as two browsers in one window, which is exactly
what this whole run of work exists to stop.

A TOKEN REMAP, NOT A NEW NUMBER. ui/views/layout/layout_provider.cc already maps
each ShapeContextTokens to a ShapeSysTokens, and resolves kMedium to 16 - the
value the expanded omnibox is already drawn at. So the four context tokens move
from kMediumSmall to kMedium and no pixel constant is introduced. Those four
were the only entries pointing at kMediumSmall, so nothing else moves with them.

Textfields were left alone. The design's inputs are 10px and kSmall is 8, but
kSmall is shared with the combobox, the find bar and the content separator, so
two pixels on a text field is not worth the blast radius.

The outlines were left alone too, and that is a decision rather than an
omission. The design puts a hairline ring on a card instead of a shadow, which
worked in CSS because a box-shadow token was already on the element; a native
menu gets its edge from views::BubbleBorder and its shadow from the compositor,
and swapping that is a much larger change with a much worse failure mode - a
menu with no visible edge against a dark window. The WebUI surfaces carry the
ring; the native ones keep their shadow.

Verified against the real 152.0.7977.82 source: the file was fetched at that
tag, edited, the patch generated from the pair, and re-applied to a clean copy -
it lands with no fuzz and reproduces the file byte for byte. Nothing else in the
series touches layout_provider.cc, checked by grepping patches/ for the
filename.

Not covered by pre-flight: layout_provider.o is not in the target list and no
path for it has been guessed, so the full build is the first compiler to see it.

